#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "capabilities-contract>=0.18,<1",
#     "httpx>=0.27",
# ]
# ///
"""telegram-bot — one Telegram bot per project: the project's agent answering in the chats
and forum topics it is allowed into, beside people, other bots and a personal account.

Author: Alexander Korenevski. MIT licence. Derived from the upstream `telegram`
capability by Cluster One (MIT; its notice is kept in LICENSE-UPSTREAM, and UPSTREAM.md
names the commit and what was taken). Where upstream signs a person's account in over MTProto, this speaks the
Bot API with a bot token, so it reads no history and lists no dialogs: what the bot knows
of a chat is what Telegram delivered to it while it ran.

Contract:
  telegram-bot help | stub | manifest --json | connections | guide [topic]
  telegram-bot ids list|get|set|rm | refs | inventory [--network]
  telegram-bot connection add|remove <id> ...   (contract; the token only on stdin)
  telegram-bot connection add <id> --remote --env secret_env=KEY --field ...
      The contract's add for a token that lives on another node (a server's
      environment): the env key is recorded without being found here.
  telegram-bot settings [get|set|unset|put|export] ...   (contract; the service settings)

Commands:
  telegram-bot doctor [--connection ID]
      Resolve the connection, then ask Telegram `getMe` and compare it with the
      connection's username and expected_bot_id. Exit 2 when the token does not
      resolve or is refused; 6 when it belongs to another bot.
  telegram-bot send <chat_id> <text> [--topic N] [--reply-to MSG] [--connection ID]
      Send one message as the bot (a write: the connection needs allow_write).
      Inside a bot turn whose authority limits telegram-bot to the current chat,
      any other chat, topic or connection is refused (exit 4), the turn's topic
      is used where --topic is left out, and --reply-to may name only the
      request the turn answers. A send that may or
      may not have arrived (a read timeout, a 5xx) is not repeated: exit 5
      `send_uncertain`, "not sure it arrived" - look in the chat before sending again.
  telegram-bot token set <id>
      Read a bot token on stdin and write it to the user's credentials.env (outside
      every project) under TELEGRAM_BOT_<ID>_TOKEN; the token is never echoed.
  telegram-bot service init [--connection ID] [--force]
      Seed the service settings in this project's records and the context prompt
      in the project's store (a context.md the project already keeps is what is
      seeded, else the template).
  telegram-bot prompts list | show <key> [--version ID] | versions <key>
  telegram-bot prompts put <key> <file|-> [--base ID] [--note TEXT]
  telegram-bot prompts import|export [--dir DIR] [--note TEXT]
      The service's prompts (`context`, read for every turn) as versions in the
      project's store, so every node reads the one the store master saved; the
      files under capabilities/telegram-bot/service/ are an optional export,
      read only while the store holds no version of a key.
  telegram-bot service doctor [--connection ID]
      Settings valid, connection resolves, getMe matches, profiles resolve, daemon state.
  telegram-bot service run|start|stop [--force]|reload|status [--connection ID]
  telegram-bot service logs [--tail N]

Connections: one per bot, each `{username, expected_bot_id, secret_env}`. The token is
never a flag or a record value: `secret_env` names the env key (default
TELEGRAM_BOT_<ID>_TOKEN), and the token lives outside every project - in
~/.config/telegram-bot/credentials.env (`token set`), or in the process environment on
a server. A token found in the project's .env.local/.env is refused, since the
project's agent can read it; so is a project `connection add --stdin`, which would
write it there. Add the connection with `--env secret_env=TELEGRAM_BOT_<ID>_TOKEN`.

Settings (records, as upstream keeps its service settings; an unknown key, an
out-of-range number or a string where a boolean belongs is refused): connection,
direct_messages {mode (allowed_users|anyone|off), default_role}, allowed_users
{<user id>: {name, role, kind, may_address, profile, brief, worker_timeout,
tail_size, control, authority}}, allowed_groups {<chat id>: {name, role,
member_role, members {<id>: {name, role, kind, may_address, control, authority}},
triggers [username, reply, command], topics {<topic id>: {name, profile, brief,
...}}, profile, brief, agent_dialogue, rate_limit, control, authority}}, control
{roles {<role>: {commands}}}, authority {default, roles {<role>:
{allowed_capabilities}}}, defaults {profile, worker_timeout, tail_size,
max_parallel, poll_timeout, progress_after, debounce, agent_dialogue {max_turns,
reset_on_human_message}, rate_limit {replies_per_minute, replies_per_minute_total}}.
A bot answers only when addressed - @username, a reply to it, /command@username - in
an allowed place by an allowed sender; another bot's bare reply is context, not a
turn; a message ending in a `#noreply` line is never a turn, and the answers to one
ending in `#external` carry `#noreply`. An addressed /command (/status, /profiles,
/stop [N], /reload, /set, /help) is answered by the daemon, gated by role, never by
the agent. What a turn may use is the sender's `authority`: seeded, a supervisor has
every capability, a direct_user none, a group_member `telegram-bot send` in the current
chat and topic and routine's read-only verbs. Every turn but a supervisor's runs on a
restricted claude profile (restricted_profile, else telegram-claude-restricted, whatever
harness the profile in force uses), with every folder outside the project denied. An
assistant on a personal account (not a bot) must be listed "kind": "agent". A turn waits
the room's debounce after the last addressed message. TELEGRAM_BOT_SERVICE_PROFILE in the
environment outranks defaults.profile, as TELEGRAM_SERVICE_PROFILE does upstream. The
daemon's state lives under $XDG_STATE_HOME/telegram-bot/projects/, outside the project.

Exit codes: 0 ok, 2 credentials/auth, 3 not found, 4 policy, 5 network/service, 6 input/config.
"""

import hashlib
